## 11. CSV EXPORT — REAL DOWNLOAD FUNCTIONALITY

Implement a working CSV export for every report type where structured tabular data is applicable.

The exported CSV must contain the complete filtered dataset, not just the currently visible page.

### CSV requirements

- Use clear column headers.
- Format values consistently.
- Preserve valid dates and numeric amounts.
- Handle commas, quotes, and line breaks in text fields.
- Use proper CSV escaping.
- Use UTF-8 encoding.
- Use a suitable filename that includes the report type and date range when possible.
- Do not export hidden sensitive fields.
- Do not silently truncate records.
- Do not export fabricated records.

Example filenames:

- `finora-transaction-report-2026-10.csv`
- `finora-income-expense-report-2026-10.csv`
- `finora-budget-performance-report.csv`
- `finora-savings-goals-report.csv`

Generate filenames dynamically based on the selected report and period.

### CSV security

Prevent spreadsheet formula injection.

Treat user-controlled text fields as untrusted. Properly handle values beginning with formula-triggering characters such as `=`, `+`, `-`, or `@`, including dangerous leading whitespace or control characters.

Apply an appropriate safe-export strategy to text fields without corrupting legitimate numeric financial values.

Do not simply concatenate raw database text into CSV rows.

Test descriptions and category names containing commas, quotes, newlines, and formula-like text.

### Download behavior

- Generate the CSV from the currently selected report and filters.
- Trigger a genuine browser download.
- Use an appropriate MIME type.
- Show useful error feedback if export fails.
- Do not display a success message unless the export operation actually completes.

Use an existing CSV library if available. Add a dependency only if necessary and compatible with the existing project.

